title: Introducing a new item type
tags: Documentation [[HyperGSD API]]

! Introducing a new item type

A checklist for sub-plugin authors (and future core) adding a new GSD item
type — say, an "event" type shipped by a hypothetical `HyperGSD-Events`.
Each join point is a tag list; membership is what makes the engine notice your
type anywhere. Nothing below assumes any other sub-plugin is present.

!! The data model, first

Before joining anything, decide:

* The **type tag** — `$:/GSD/Event`. It goes on every user tiddler of the type
  and is the value other tag lists point at (`hgsd-type-tag` fields).
* The **fields** your type owns — user-item fields take the `gsd_` prefix
  (`gsd_venue`). Never an unprefixed field; plugin-owned tiddlers use
  `hgsd-` instead.
* The **tags your type carries beyond its type tag** — a caller's other
  tags (`chess`, `work`) flow onto new items created from its panel
  automatically (see
  [[Inheritance|Inheritance]]); anything
  structural you add yourself (a realm tag, a context tag) must come through
  its own mechanism, never by asking users to tag by hand.
* Whether the type **takes a status** — only action and project do today.
  Status machinery (status sets, the per-type memory field) is join-point one
  below; skip it all if not.

!! Join points, in build order

# ''The Type tag list'' — a member tiddler tagged `$:/tags/hypergsd/Type`
  with `hgsd-type: event`. This single join makes creation, conversion and
  every type-gated surface recognise the type. If the type takes a status,
  also join the relevant status-set tag lists and set a default status.
# ''ViewPlacement'' — a member of `$:/tags/hypergsd/ViewPlacement` with
  `hgsd-type-tag: $:/GSD/Event` and a config/default side, so the type's
  view panels render above and below the body. Without it the type renders
  bare.
# ''Panel sections'' — members of `$:/tags/hypergsd/PanelTemplate` (with a
  Portion/''n'' claim) render in the view panel; the same tiddlers tagged
  `$:/tags/hypergsd/ModalTemplate` (with its own Portion claim) render as the
  new-item modal's attribute rows. Dual-context sections read
  `section-target`/`section-type`/`section-mode` instead of hard-coding
  `currentTiddler`.
# ''List blocks'' — for each dashboard you want the type listed on, ship a
  member of that dashboard's tag list with `hgsd-heading` and a Portion
  claim. Visibility rides the dashboard's visibility base; order rides the
  tag's `list` field (users reorder via Settings).
# ''Capture'' — a member of a `$:/tags/hypergsd/Capture/Block/''n''` list so
  the type's capture controls appear in the capture sidebar, plus a toolbar
  button (`$hgsd.new-button`) for creating it from anywhere.
# ''Inheritance'' — only if the type needs a rule at creation time (see the
  [[Inheritance docs|Inheritance]]).
  Tag inheritance is core and free (Settings → Behaviour carries the
  user's kill switch); your rule exists only for a deliberate system-tag or
  creation-time relationship.
# ''AfterCreation'' — only for post-creation side effects
  (`$:/tags/hypergsd/AfterCreation`; navigation is core's).

!! Things that need no join

* The type tag itself arrives on created items from the Type tag list —
  `create-gsd-tiddler` resolves it from `hgsd-type`.
* The modal, conversion and the list widget are type-agnostic; they read the
  joins above.
* Every Settings drag list names its own tag basis automatically: the note
  "Tiddlers tagged with ⟪pill⟫" (plus the portions sentence where the rows
  carry a Portion picker) comes from `hgsd-visibility-list` — nothing to
  register, and third-party lists get it too.
* Docs are plain tiddlers; nothing registers them.

!! Removal semantics for everything you ship

Every tag list here is a plain tag scan — users disable a shipped member by
editing it and removing its tag (the edit supersedes the shadow; deleting
their tiddler restores it). Tag lists with a `list` field pin order only:
an untagged member drops out even though its title lingers in the list. Do
not build a join point that requires deleting a plugin tiddler — shadows
cannot be deleted.